course: dictionary
lesson: 10051
title: Words that show great fear
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Terror» is a noun. It names a very great fear, the kind that makes you freeze. The words around it give the clue: «They screamed in terror» tells you the fear was so strong that people cried out.
explain: Terror can also mean someone or something that causes great fear. In «Toad the terror, the traffic-queller», the words around terror show that Toad is a bold, frightening character who makes others fear him.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If terror means very great fear, does «They screamed in terror» still make sense? Yes, so your meaning fits.
explain: Be careful with «a little terror». In family talk it is a joking name for a naughty child, not a frightening person. Words like «little» and «naughty» show it is a fond joke.
fact: Terror is a noun, and it is usually uncountable when it means the feeling: you feel terror, not "a terror".
fact: «Terror» means very great fear.
fact: «Terror» can also mean violence used to frighten people, usually for political aims. This is called terrorism. If news about it worries you, talk to a trusted adult.
fact: «A little terror» is a jokey way to describe a naughty child.
fact: «Screamed» makes a sound wild and frightening, like a person in terror.
exercise: «They screamed in terror.» What does terror mean here? | Great joy at a party | A loud noise from a band | *Very great fear | A type of shoe
exercise: Mum laughs and says, «Their toddler is a little terror!» What does she mean? | A tiny bit of fear | A grown-up who is very kind | A frightening monster under the bed | *A naughty, wild child
exercise: In «Toad the terror, the traffic-queller», what does terror mean? | A bright light | *Someone who causes great fear | A small grey bird | A feeling of great joy
test: Terror can mean violence used to frighten people for political aims. What is this called? | Terrier | Territory | *Terrorism | Tornado
test: In family talk, what does «a little terror» mean? | A sweet small pet | *A naughty child | A scary film | A wise old grandpa
exam: Priya says terror always means a tiny worry. Is she right? | No, it means a sunny day | Yes, it is a kind of food | *No, terror means very great fear | Yes, it is only a tiny worry
